# Profile: sergiy_prod
#
# Per-user values for ONE canonical repo. NO SECRETS LIVE HERE — this file is committed.
# Secrets stay in ~/.config/ai-agent-stack/secrets.env (0600), which is never in git.
#
# Everything below is either a render substitution (scripts/render.sh) or a port/path
# assignment that must differ between the two runtimes sharing this box.

# ── identity / render tokens ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
PROFILE_USER=sergiy_prod
PROFILE_HOME=/home/sergiy_prod
PROFILE_DEST=/srv/sergiy_prod/ai-agents-config
# Display name the agent uses when it addresses the owner, and the GitHub account.
# These replace @OWNER@ / @GH_OWNER@, which are read AS INSTRUCTIONS out of SOUL.md.
PROFILE_OWNER=Sergiy
PROFILE_GH_OWNER=SergeMiro
# Where this owner's projects live. Content does not sit under /srv with the system,
# so there is no author path to swap — the @PROJECT_ROOT@ token carries the answer.
PROFILE_PROJECT_ROOT=/home/sergiy_prod/workspaces

# ── ports: the whole reason a profile exists ─────────────────────────────────
# Two runtimes on one box, one loopback. Every port below is bound for real, so a
# shared value means the second service to start silently loses — that is exactly how
# the conductor webhook lost every Approve/Deny tap on the shared 3001 default.
PROFILE_HO_WEBHOOK_PORT=3011
PROFILE_QDRANT_HTTP_PORT=6343
PROFILE_QDRANT_GRPC_PORT=6344
PROFILE_PROXY_AGENTIC_PORT=47821
PROFILE_PROXY_STRONG_PORT=47822

# ── conductor state ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Runtime state belongs in ~/.hermes, not inside a project checkout: a project repo
# gets cloned, moved and cleaned, and the queue history must not travel with it.
PROFILE_CONDUCTOR_DB=/home/sergiy_prod/.hermes/ho.db

# ── service topology ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which systemd scope each unit lives in. NOT cosmetic: the two profiles disagree
# today — here the conductor is a SYSTEM unit while gateway/qdrant/proxies are user
# units, and on vadim_prod every one of them is a user unit. Any script that
# starts/stops/queries a service has to know which, or it silently talks to the wrong
# scope and reports "inactive" about something that is running fine.
#
# There is also a DISABLED user unit for hermes-conductor still on disk here. It is a
# trap, not a spare: enable it and two conductors claim jobs from one ho.db. doctor.sh
# checks for exactly that.
PROFILE_UNIT_SCOPE_CONDUCTOR=system
PROFILE_UNIT_SCOPE_DEFAULT=user

# ── role ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CONSUMER, like every other profile. This tree used to be the canonical git repo AND the
# runtime at once, which meant an editor and a production system shared one directory: saving a
# half-finished edit made it the live config that Claude Code and the conductor immediately read.
# `update.sh --force` would also `reset --hard` over real work rather than over render output.
#
# Development moved to PROFILE_DEV_TREE below. The runtime path did NOT move — two systemd units
# and all six DEV/profiles/*.json name it absolutely, so relocating it would have been a
# migration with downtime for no benefit. What changed is only where commits happen.
PROFILE_ROLE=consumer

# Where this account DEVELOPS the repo. Not a runtime; nothing reads it at run time. Deliberately
# under /srv and not ~/workspaces, because repo-manager.sh `rm -rf`s a workspace directory after
# 72 hours of inactivity — a dev checkout would eventually be deleted from under you.
#
# Workflow: edit and commit here, push, then `update.sh` in the runtime tree pulls it.
PROFILE_DEV_TREE=/srv/sergiy_prod/ai-agents-config-dev
